 "about": "Every model Hearth Forge can use (docs/mockups/hearth-forge/02-models.png, the owner's approved board of Nov 26, 2026). What each makes, the memory it needs and whether it fits 12 GB are each maker's own figures (Nov 2026); the licence is the maker's. Weights come only from each model's own Hugging Face repo, only when you press Get: every file is checked against the SHA-256 Hugging Face lists for it at the pinned revision (\"revision\"; empty means Hearth pins the commit Hugging Face names at your first Get and keeps to it, in ~/Hearth/Forge/models/forge-lock.json). \"runner\" is the model's own code Hearth Forge makes it with (runners below): the maker's repo at a pinned git commit, with the maker's own pinned requirements (\"requirements\", minus \"skip\": demo-only parts and anything that would swap the PyTorch build), in its own Python environment in ~/Hearth/Forge/runtimes/<runner>, installed on Get; PyTorch is always the CUDA 12.8 build (the RTX 5070 needs it) and every git requirement must name a commit. \"paint\": a shape-only model's textures painted by Hunyuan3D 2.0's painter (when it's got). \"coarse\": the models a refiner (Hi3D, UltraShape) can start from. \"scan\": the model's own config files that name the other Hugging Face repos it loads (got too, checked the same way). Built into the app (and its Store updates): one copy, read by the app and by hearth-forge on the Nvidia PC.",
